{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Github Copilot (GHCP) Workshop - Playground 25 (C#)",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "../GHCP-dotNet.code-workspace",
      "description": "# From Prompt Engineering to Context Engineering\r\n\r\n## Utilize VS Code workspaces\r\n\r\n1. Setup your Copilot instructions for test creation, code generation, code-reviews and even commit messages.\r\n2. Commit the VS Code workspace in your repo and instruct your team to use it for development.\r\n3. Instructions come in the form of free text in markdown files. The more context you enrich Github Copilot with, the better results you'll get.\r\n\r\nSee [docs](https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/how-tos/configure-custom-instructions/add-repository-instructions#creating-repository-wide-custom-instructions)",
      "pattern": "github.copilot.chat.commitMessageGeneration.instructions"
    },
    {
      "title": "Create a Custom Chat Mode: PRD builder",
      "description": "Let's set up our own custom GitHub Copilot Chat Mode to accelerate creation of Product Requirements Documents (PRDs).\n\n**Goal**: Create a mode named **PRD builder** that loads the persona instructions so you can quickly draft structured PRDs from short prompts.\n\n### Steps\n1. Open the Copilot Chat panel (Ctrl + Alt + I).\n1. Click the mode dropdown (where you pick *Ask* / *Agent*). You probably noticed there are already two custom modes, let's create our own by selecting **Configure modes** (see screenshot below).\n\n    ![Configure Modes](./assets/ConfigureModes.png)\n\n1. In the configuration UI, click **Create a new custom mode**.\n\n    ![Create Mode](./assets/Create a new custom mode.png)\n\n1. Let's use the User level scope to share this across projects instead of being project specific\n\n    ![User Data Folder](./assets/UserDataFolder.png)    \n        \n1. Name it exactly: `PRD builder`.\n1. Open this file `PRD-Builder-Persona.md` ([link](./personas/PRD-Builder-Persona.md)) and copy its entire contents into the new mode's definition/instructions text area.\n1. (Optional) Pick an emoji/icon to make it stand out (🧱, 📄, 🚀).\n1. Save the mode.\n\nCongratulations, you've created a custom mode.\n\n\n### What This Gives You\n- Consistent PRD structure (intro, goals, user stories, requirements, non-goals, metrics)\n- Automatic reminder to ask clarifying questions first\n- Faster onboarding for teammates who can reuse the same mode\n\n### Pro Tips\n- You can store multiple personas (e.g., *Test Plan Builder*, *API Spec Writer*) the same way.\n- Version your persona files in Git so the team can improve them collaboratively.\n- If you update the persona text here, re-open the mode config and re-paste to sync.\n\nWhen you're done, try giving it a *very short* feature idea and see how it first asks you clarifying questions (that's by design!).\n\nGive it a spin—your future specs will thank you. ✨"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Program.cs",
      "title": "Putting it all together",
      "description": "Now let's experience the complete workflow using both custom chat modes you've created. This demonstrates the full cycle from idea to implementation-ready tasks.\n\n## 🚀 Complete Workflow: From Idea to Implementation\n\n### Step 1: Create a PRD using PRD Builder Mode\n\n1. **Open Copilot Chat** (Ctrl + Alt + I)\n2. **Select the PRD builder mode** from the dropdown\n3. **Enter this prompt exactly:**\n   > Draft a PRD for a feature: Create a console app that displays the initial list of cars and has filteration options which returns partial cars.\n\n4. **Follow the conversation flow:**\n   - The mode will ask you clarifying questions. This is the important part of the process, it SHOULD take time! This is equivalent to the feature design phase.\n   - You can respond with short answers or say \"Skip for now, make the decisions for me. I'd like to run and demo this ASAP\"\n   - The mode will generate a comprehensive PRD and save it to the `/tasks/` directory\n\n**Expected Result:** A detailed PRD file will be created (e.g., `tasks/prd-car-filter-console.md`) \n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Convert PRD to Tasks using PRD to Tasks Mode\n\n1. **Switch to the PRD to Tasks mode** in the same chat panel\n2. **Reference the PRD file created in Step 1:**\n   > Generate tasks from the created PRD file\n\n3. **Follow the two-phase process:**\n   - **Phase 1:** Review the high-level parent tasks (usually ~5 tasks)\n   - **Phase 2:** When prompted, respond with \"Go\" to generate detailed sub-tasks\n   - The mode will create a structured task list with checkboxes\n\n**Expected Result:** A task file will be created (e.g., `tasks/tasks-prd-car-filter-console.md`)\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Review Generated Tasks\n\n1. **Open the generated task file** from the `/tasks/` directory\n2. **Review the structure:**\n   - Relevant Files section (lists files to be created/modified)\n   - Parent tasks (numbered 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, etc.)\n   - Sub-tasks (numbered 1.1, 1.2, 2.1, 2.2, etc.)\n   - Each task has a checkbox for tracking progress\n\n3. **Verify the tasks make sense** for the PRD requirements\n4. **Note the file structure** suggested in the \"Relevant Files\" section\n\n**Checkpoint:** You now have both a comprehensive PRD and an actionable task list! ✅\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Start Processing Tasks\n\n1. **Switch back to Agent mode** in Copilot Chat\n2. **Begin implementation** by referencing the task file:\n   > Let's start implementing the car filter console app. Begin with the first task.\n\n3. **Work through tasks systematically:**\n   - Copilot will create the necessary files\n   - Check off completed tasks in your task file\n   - Reference the PRD for detailed requirements when needed\n\n4. **Test as you go:**\n   - Run `dotnet run` to test the console app\n   - Verify functionality matches the PRD requirements\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 What You've Accomplished\n\nBy completing this workflow, you've experienced:\n\n✅ **Structured Requirements Gathering** - From a simple idea to a detailed PRD\n✅ **Task Decomposition** - Breaking down complex features into actionable steps\n✅ **Implementation Planning** - Clear file structure and development roadmap\n✅ **Custom Mode Integration** - Seamless workflow between specialized AI assistants\n\n## 💡 Pro Tips for Your Team\n\n- **Standardize this workflow** across your development team\n- **Reference PRDs during code reviews** to ensure requirements are met\n- **Iterate on your personas** - improve them based on real project experience\n- **Create additional modes** for other workflows (API design, test planning, etc.)\n\n## 🔄 Workflow Summary\n```\nIdea → PRD Builder → Detailed PRD → PRD to Tasks → Task List → Agent Mode → Implementation\n```\n\nThis approach ensures nothing falls through the cracks and provides clear documentation for your entire development process!",
      "pattern": "Program"
    },
    {
      "description": "## 🔒 Security Scanning: Finding Exposed Secrets\n\nOne of the most critical security concerns in codebases is accidentally committed secrets like API keys, passwords, or tokens. Let's use GitHub Copilot to help us identify potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n### Your Mission: Find Hidden Secrets\n\n**Goal**: Use Copilot to scan the codebase for potentially exposed secrets, credentials, or sensitive information.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. **Open GitHub Copilot Chat** (Ctrl + Alt + I)\n2. **Switch to Agent mode** if not already selected\n1. **Setup the context** Close all editors\n3. **Use this security-focused prompt:**\n   > Search #codebase for secrets\n\n4. **Follow up with specific questions:**\n   \n### 🎯 Expected Discovery\n\nCopilot should identify security issues in this file, including:\n- Exposed API keys\n- Commented-out sensitive information\n- Poor security practices\n\n### 🛡️ What This Demonstrates\n\n**Proactive Security**: Instead of waiting for security scans in CI/CD, catch issues during development\n\n**Pattern Recognition**: Copilot can identify various secret patterns and naming conventions\n\n**Code Review Enhancement**: Use this as part of your PR review process\n\n### 💡 Pro Security Tips\n\n- **Never commit real secrets** - use environment variables or secure vaults\n- **Use .gitignore** for files containing sensitive data\n- **Implement pre-commit hooks** to scan for secrets before commits\n- **Regular security audits** using tools like this Copilot workflow\n\n### 🚨 Action Items After Discovery\n\nOnce Copilot finds issues:\n1. **Remove the secrets** from the code\n2. **Add them to .gitignore** if they're in config files\n3. **Use environment variables** or Azure Key Vault\n4. **Rotate any exposed credentials** immediately\n5. **Review git history** to see if secrets were previously committed\n\nTry the scan now - you might be surprised what Copilot finds! 🔍",
      "title": "Security Scan: Finding Exposed Secrets"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/LegacyCodeBase.cs",
      "description": "Here's is a snippet of a legacy code base. It's full of ToDos, and code which could be improved. \r\n\r\nLet's use Copilot to ease our assessment of technical debt.\r\n\r\nOpen Github Copilot in **`Ask`** Mode. And write the following prompt\r\n\r\n> Gather all the todos from the file in the format of a table, add a column for the the content, add priority, and T-shirt size cost estimation",
      "pattern": "public class SmartHouseManager",
      "title": "Extracting info"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/LegacyCodeBase.cs",
      "description": "Now let's switch `Agent` mode and ask to to implement the first to do item:\r\n\r\n> Implement the To-Dos start with the highest priority and work downwards.",
      "pattern": "TODO Add my fridge",
      "title": "Implementation task"
    }
  ]
}